<p>A form is a named container that lives on the <code>Document</code>, and fields are added to a <code>Page</code>. Each
field names the form it belongs to, and when the document compiles, every field that names a
registered form is written as a widget annotation and its object number is indexed into that form.</p>
<p><strong>Disclaimer</strong></p>
<p>Form support varies widely by reader — Acrobat implements the full field and flag set, while browser
viewers, OS previewers and mobile apps each implement a subset. Pop PDF writes a field's structure and
leaves the drawing of it to the reader, so test any form in the readers your audience uses.</p>
<h2 id="creating-a-form">Creating a Form<a class="anchor" href="#creating-a-form" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>

<p><code>Document\Form</code> takes one argument, the name, and <code>addForm()</code> puts it on the document:</p>
<div class="code-window" x-data="copyCode">
<div class="code-window-bar"><span class="code-lang">PHP</span><button type="button" class="code-copy" @click="copy()" :aria-label="copied ? 'Copied' : 'Copy code'" aria-label="Copy code"><svg x-show="!copied" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="13" height="13" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="9" y="9" width="11.5" height="11.5" rx="2.4"></rect><path d="M15 5.6A2.6 2.6 0 0 0 12.4 3H5.6A2.6 2.6 0 0 0 3 5.6v6.8A2.6 2.6 0 0 0 5.6 15"></path></svg><svg x-show="copied" x-cloak class="code-copied" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="13" height="13" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4.5 12.5 9 17l10.5-10.5"></path></svg></button></div>
<pre><code><span class="text-kw">use</span> <span class="text-type">Pop\Pdf\Document</span>;
<span class="text-kw">use</span> <span class="text-type">Pop\Pdf\Document\Form</span>;
<span class="text-kw">use</span> <span class="text-type">Pop\Pdf\Document\Page</span>;

<span class="text-var">$form</span> = <span class="text-kw">new</span> <span class="text-type">Form</span>(<span class="text-str">&#039;signup&#039;</span>);

<span class="text-var">$document</span> = <span class="text-kw">new</span> <span class="text-type">Document</span>(<span class="text-kw">new</span> <span class="text-type">Page</span>(<span class="text-type">Page</span>::<span class="text-num">LETTER</span>));
<span class="text-var">$document</span>-&gt;<span class="text-fn">addForm</span>(<span class="text-var">$form</span>);
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>Forms are keyed on the document by name:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>getForm('signup')</code> hands back the instance you added; <code>getForm()</code> returns <code>null</code> for a name never
registered</li>
<li><code>getForms()</code> returns the whole map, keyed by name</li>
<li><code>hasForms()</code> reports whether the document carries any</li>
<li>a document with at least one form compiles a catalog carrying an <code>/AcroForm</code> reference; one that
never sees <code>addForm()</code> compiles <code>&lt;&lt;/Pages 2 0 R/Type/Catalog&gt;&gt;</code> and writes no form dictionary</li>
</ul>
<p><code>getFieldIndices()</code> and <code>getNumberOfFields()</code> read the indices the compiler wrote. Both are empty
until the document is compiled, because a field's object number is not known until the objects around
it have been laid out:</p>
